Topological Defect Spectroscopy
— Geometric Mechanism of Characteristic Spectral Lines within the TDMU Framework
TDMU Spectroscopy Supplement v1.2

Author: mjp

Abstract
Within the axiomatic system of the Topological Dynamical Membrane Universe (TDMU v2.342), this paper proposes a purely geometric interpretation of characteristic spectra. Core thesis: the atomic nucleus is not an assembly of particles, but a continuous topological knot woven layer by layer from primitive bricks. The weaving scars (healed defect-complementarity interfaces) and topological defects (unclosed dangling ports) left behind during this weaving process jointly constitute the geometric origin of spectral activity. Light—the transverse wave perturbation of shared link orientation, δ𝒜—undergoes scattering, capture, and resonant release when traversing these geometrically inhomogeneous regions. The spectral line frequency is jointly determined by the depth of the scar within the weave body and the stress state at that location; the line width is determined by the smoothness of the scar's healed surface; the total number of spectral lines is jointly determined by the total number of scars and defects within the depth reachable by the light's energy. This paper applies this unified mechanism to systems such as hydrogen, iron, diamond, and mercury, and discusses the geometric interpretation of chemical shifts and molecular spectra. All concepts are expressed using native TDMU terminology, without recourse to standard-model concepts such as "energy levels," "transitions," or "electron orbits."

1. The Geometric Origin of Spectral Activity
1.1 The Weaving Structure of the Atomic Nucleus
In TDMU, the atomic nucleus is not an assembly of protons and neutrons, but a continuous topological knot woven layer by layer from primitive bricks. There are no neutrons, no protons, no electrons—these are merely convenient labels for stable topological knots at different orders along the recursive growth path. Each step of recursive weaving is carried out upon an already existing structure. There are no interfaces between independent substructures, only the scars left behind by the weaving. The primitive brick is the starting point of weaving. Its structure was fixed at the moment of membrane emergence. Subsequent weaving does not alter the primitive brick itself, but the layer-by-layer wrapping continuously modifies the stress state of the environment surrounding the primitive brick.

1.2 Two Types of Geometrically Inhomogeneous Regions
During this weaving process, two types of geometrically inhomogeneous regions emerge.

Definition 1.1: Weaving Scar

After each step of defect-complementarity weaving is completed, a healed but geometrically non-ideal region of the shared link network forms on the meshing interface. This is called a weaving scar. Its characteristics: the link saturation has reached a local maximum (the weave is closed; it is no longer a "hole"); the smoothness is inferior to that of an ideal weave surface—the shared link orientation tensor on the sutured surface deviates from a uniform arrangement, and residual topological stress exists; the local stiffness differs from that of the surrounding ideal weave region, constituting an impedance mismatch interface. A weaving scar is a structural imprint of the recursive weaving history inside the topological knot.

Definition 1.2: Topological Defect

An unsaturated dangling port left on the surface of a weave body where the weave cannot achieve absolutely perfect closure is called a topological defect. A topological defect is an active site for nuclear reactions (the trigger position for Pathway I) and simultaneously an active site for spectral absorption.

Distinction between the two types of inhomogeneous regions:

Weaving Scar	Topological Defect
Location	Inside the weave body	Outermost surface of the weave body
Link State	Saturated and closed	Unsaturated (dangling ports exist)
Smoothness	Non-ideally smooth (healing traces exist)	Not applicable (it is an open boundary)
Spectral Role	Impedance mismatch → scattering and resonance	Absorption (breaking the local constraint of the dangling link)
Nuclear Reaction Role	None (already saturated)	Present (trigger site for Pathway I)
1.3 Linkage Zone: A Collective Term for Both Types of Inhomogeneous Regions
Definition 1.3: Linkage Zone

A general term encompassing both weaving scars and topological defects. A linkage zone is a localized region within the weave body where the shared link network exhibits inhomogeneity—whether a healed scar or an unclosed defect, their common feature is that the local link state differs detectably from the surrounding ideal weave region.

A linkage zone possesses three geometric parameters that directly determine spectral characteristics:

Parameter	Definition	Spectral Correspondence
Depth	The spatial position of the linkage zone within the weave body (measured in node steps from the nuclear surface)	Primary determinant of resonance/absorption frequency
Degree of Stability	The average link saturation 𝒮 of the shared links within the linkage zone (high for saturated scars, low for unsaturated defects)	Secondary determinant of resonance/absorption frequency
Smoothness	The geometric deviation of the scar's healed surface from the ideal weave surface; the boundary irregularity of a defect	Spectral line width
Proposition 1 (Spectral Line Frequency): The spectral line frequency is jointly determined by the depth of the linkage zone and the stress state at that location. Greater depth and higher degree of stability → greater local stiffness → higher resonance frequency. However, local stiffness is not an isolated property—the global stress distribution of the entire weave body can compress or stretch scars in specific regions, altering their resonance frequencies. Therefore, any given spectral line is a comprehensive manifestation of the stress equilibrium state of the entire weave body at the location of that scar.

Proposition 2 (Spectral Line Width): The spectral line width is determined by the smoothness of the scar. Lower smoothness (rougher healed surface, more irregular defect boundary) → greater rate of excited-state energy dissipation → broader line width.

Proposition 3 (Total Number of Spectral Lines): Total number of spectral lines = within the depth range reachable by the light's energy, the total number of weaving scars (contributing resonant lines) + the total number of topological defects (contributing absorption lines).

1.4 Interaction Mechanism between Light and Linkage Zones
In TDMU, light is defined as the transverse wave perturbation of shared link orientation, δ𝒜.

When δ𝒜 propagates through the shared link network and encounters a linkage zone:

Impedance Mismatch: The local stiffness of the linkage zone differs from that of the surroundings → a portion of the perturbation is reflected (scattering).

Resonant Capture: If the linkage zone forms an effective resonant cavity (with boundary conditions determined by the suture line geometry), δ𝒜 of a specific frequency is captured and oscillates at the cavity's natural frequency.

Resonant Release: The captured energy is released through tunneling or boundary reconnection dissipation → emission spectral line.

Absorption: If the linkage zone is a topological defect (unsaturated dangling port), the energy of the incident δ𝒜 is absorbed to break the local constraint of that port → absorption spectral line.

Herein lies the geometric mechanism for both absorption and emission spectra.

2. Hydrogen: The Weave Body with the Shortest Weaving History
2.1 Geometric Structure
The hydrogen nucleus is the lowest-order stable topological knot on the recursive growth path in the TDMU framework—starting from the primitive brick, it has undergone the fewest steps of recursive weaving. Any recursive weaving produces a multi-layered structure. Even the hydrogen nucleus contains splicing interfaces between primitive bricks (weaving scars) inside and unsaturated dangling ports (topological defects) on its surface. These linkage zones are distributed across various depths from the core center to the surface.

Geometric features of the hydrogen nucleus: the fewest recursive steps → the shortest weaving history; an extremely small total number of linkage zones—yet they exist at every depth (deep: brick-splicing scars; intermediate: interlayer interface scars; surface: weave-closure scars and surface defects); the fewest weaving steps → minimal cumulative weaving error → extremely high smoothness for all linkage zones.

2.2 Spectral Facts
The hydrogen atomic spectrum consists of several series (Lyman, Balmer, Paschen, etc.), with frequencies satisfying the Rydberg formula. The number of spectral lines is relatively small, and the line widths are extremely narrow. The Lyman series lies in the ultraviolet band (91–122 nm), the Balmer series in the visible band (364–656 nm), and the Paschen series in the infrared band (820–1875 nm). Typical natural line widths are on the order of 10⁻⁵ nm.

2.3 TDMU Interpretation
Small number of spectral lines: The total number of linkage zones is extremely small. However, linkage zones exist at every depth layer, so the hydrogen spectrum still presents multiple series spanning UV to IR.

Extremely narrow line widths: The fewest weaving steps → minimal cumulative weaving error → extremely high smoothness for all scars → extremely slow excited-state energy dissipation → very sharp lines.

Integer-ratio relationship of frequencies: Harmonic structure of resonance modes in a finite-scale linkage zone—each linkage zone, acting as a resonant cavity, supports a fundamental frequency and a series of integer-multiple overtones. This is a universal mathematical property of isolated, finite-boundary resonant structures.

Different series correspond to different depths: The Lyman series corresponds to deep linkage zone resonance (high frequency, short wavelength); the Balmer series corresponds to intermediate-depth linkage zone resonance; the Paschen series corresponds to shallow linkage zone resonance (low frequency, long wavelength). The wavelength differences among the three series reflect the systematic change in linkage zone depth.

Regarding the Rydberg formula: The 1/n² form of the Rydberg formula is interpreted in this framework as the harmonic structure of the linkage zone resonant cavity. In the standard model, n is the principal quantum number; in this framework, n corresponds to the harmonic order of the resonance mode. The two are mathematically equivalent but differ in physical ontology. This framework cannot derive the precise numerical value of the Rydberg constant from first principles—it remains a parameter to be determined.

Regarding selection rules: This framework currently cannot derive an equivalent selection rule from its geometric mechanism. Preliminary speculation: selection rules may originate from the matching condition between the polarization direction of the incident light δ𝒜 and the geometric symmetry of the resonant cavity—only modes whose symmetry axis matches the polarization can be effectively excited.

Conclusion: The simple spectrum of hydrogen is a direct consequence of being "the weave body with the shortest weaving history, possessing an extremely small total number of linkage zones, yet with zones distributed at all depths."

3. Iron: The Rich Scar System of Deep Recursive Weaving
3.1 Geometric Structure
The iron nucleus (predominantly Fe-56) is a giant stable topological knot formed from primitive bricks through a large number of recursive weaving steps. Its recursive volume far exceeds that of the hydrogen nucleus: its weaving history is complex, with each step leaving scars; linkage zones are distributed across all depths from surface to core, with the number at each depth far exceeding that of hydrogen; the distribution of linkage zones across depths is relatively uniform—a natural consequence of multi-step weaving, where each weaving stage contributes scars at corresponding depths.

3.2 Spectral Facts
The visible spectrum of iron is exceptionally line-rich (tens of thousands of recorded lines in the visible band alone), covering an extremely broad band from infrared to hard X-rays. Fe-56 is one of the most stable nuclides in the universe (highest binding energy per nucleon). Neighboring nuclides (Fe-54, Fe-57, Fe-58) possess nearly identical chemical properties but significantly different nuclear properties.

3.3 TDMU Interpretation
Extremely rich spectrum: The total number of weaving scars and topological defects in the iron nucleus is vast. Each scar corresponds to a set of resonant modes, and each defect corresponds to an absorption feature. The tens of thousands of lines are the superposition of independent contributions from a multitude of linkage zones.

Broad band coverage: Linkage zones are distributed relatively uniformly across all depths—from surface to core, each depth has a sufficient number of linkage zones to respond to light of the corresponding energy scale. Surface scars produce low-frequency lines (IR–visible), while deep scars produce high-frequency lines (X-rays). This aligns with the multi-step weaving history of the iron nucleus.

Extreme nuclear stability: Although numerous surface and mid-level linkage zones exist (producing the rich spectrum), the deep weaving regions of the iron nucleus have highly ordered shared link arrangements, with link saturation approaching the maximum and suture line stress concentration factor extremely low. This is the geometric root of the iron nucleus possessing the highest compressive binding energy.

No contradiction: Linkage zones at different depths are independently probed by light of different energy scales. Low-energy light (visible) only reaches surface linkage zones; high-energy light (X-rays) is needed to probe deep linkage zones. The richness of the surface spectrum and the stability of the deep structure are carried by completely independent geometric degrees of freedom and do not conflict.

Conclusion: The extreme complexity of the iron spectrum is a direct result of "the iron nucleus possessing a vast number of weaving scars distributed across all depths." The extreme stability of the iron nucleus is a result of "near-perfect deep weaving." The two are not contradictory.

4. Diamond: Saturation Pairing of All Linkage Zones
4.1 Geometric Structure
In a diamond crystal, the topological defects on the surface of each carbon nucleus undergo saturation pairing with the corresponding defects of adjacent carbon nuclei through Pathway I (defect-complementarity weaving). The entire crystal forms a macroscopic, rigid-body shared link network with no isolated linkage zones. All surface defects of carbon nuclei are saturation-paired → no isolated unsaturated defects. Weaving scars still exist, but the defect-complementarity weaving causes the scars of adjacent nuclei to interlock, forming a globally rigid link network spanning the whole crystal. Link lengths are highly uniform throughout the crystal.

4.2 Spectral Facts
Diamond exhibits no characteristic atomic spectral lines. It shows only a single collective lattice vibration peak (first-order Raman peak at 1332 cm⁻¹) with extremely narrow width (~2 cm⁻¹). The absorption edge is steep (indirect band gap ~5.5 eV). It is transparent in the visible to near-infrared region, with no detectable atomic absorption or emission lines.

4.3 TDMU Interpretation
No characteristic atomic spectral lines: All topological defects have been saturation-paired; no isolated dangling ports exist for light absorption. Although weaving scars exist inside the carbon nuclei, their independent resonance is suppressed by the rigid global shared link network—scars cannot operate as independent resonant cavities because their boundary conditions are locked by the rigid coupling of adjacent scars.

Single lattice vibration peak: The global shared link network of the entire crystal possesses only one fundamental collective vibration mode—all links stretching and contracting in phase. 1332 cm⁻¹ is the characteristic frequency of this collective mode.

Extremely sharp peak: Link lengths are highly consistent throughout the crystal, resulting in extremely low energy dissipation for the collective vibration mode.

Steep absorption edge: 5.5 eV corresponds to the minimum energy threshold required to break a link within the globally rigid link network. Below this energy, no dissipative channel is available.

Conclusion: Diamond is spectroscopically "silent"—all surface defects are saturation-paired with adjacent carbon nuclei, and the independent resonance of internal scars is suppressed by the globally rigid network. Light can only perceive the collective vibration of the crystal.

5. Mercury: Collective Freezing of Linkage Zones
5.1 Geometric Structure
Mercury is liquid at room temperature. The relative positions of atomic nuclei constantly change, and the linkage zones of each nucleus are in a thermally activated state—vibrating independently with no global phase correlation. When the temperature drops below the superconducting transition temperature, mercury enters the superconducting state. In the TDMU framework, the superconducting state corresponds to the combinatorial covariant derivative reducing to zero for all nearest-neighbor node pairs—that is, all linkage zones achieve global phase synchronization, with the helical motions of all shared links perfectly synchronous.

5.2 Spectral Facts
In the normal state, far-infrared reflectivity is flat, around 90%. In the superconducting state, far-infrared reflectivity jumps abruptly to nearly 100% at a characteristic frequency, with a weak absorption dip at the threshold frequency. The far-infrared reflectivity of superconducting mercury jumps from ~90% to >99.9% for frequencies below about 10¹¹ Hz, with an absorption threshold appearing at roughly 3×10¹¹ Hz.

5.3 TDMU Interpretation
Normal state: The linkage zones of different nuclei vibrate independently (thermally activated). Incident light δ𝒜 may encounter impedance mismatch at each linkage zone → partial scattering, partial absorption and dissipation → moderate reflectivity, no sharp spectral features.

Superconducting state: All linkage zones enter a globally phase-locked state. In this state, incident light δ𝒜 with frequency below a certain threshold cannot excite any asynchronous perturbation in the shared link network—because the motion of all links is locked, δ𝒜 must break this global lock to be absorbed. Low-frequency light cannot break the lock → it is completely reflected, with reflectivity approaching 100%.

Threshold frequency: Corresponds to the minimum energy required to break the global lock. At this threshold, the incident light can just excite linkage zones out of the synchronous state, creating an absorption dip. The threshold frequency is phenomenologically equivalent to the superconducting energy gap.

Conclusion: The superconducting phase transition is a collective synchronization transition of all linkage zones. In the normal state, zones scatter light independently. In the superconducting state, low-frequency light encounters a fully synchronized, rigid shared link network, cannot be absorbed, and is totally reflected.

6. Chemical Shift and Molecular Spectra
6.1 Chemical Shift
When atoms bond to form molecules, the topological defects on the surfaces of two nuclei couple through Pathway I, forming new shared link channels (chemical bonds). This coupling modifies the boundary conditions of the participating linkage zones: compressed (link shortened) → local stiffness increases → scar resonance frequency rises → spectral line blue shift; stretched (link elongated) → local stiffness decreases → scar resonance frequency drops → spectral line red shift. Spectral frequency shifts caused by changes in chemical environment are widely observed experimentally. This framework interprets this phenomenon as: the geometric constraints of different chemical bonds alter the effective stiffness of the participating scars.

6.2 New Features in Molecular Spectra
After molecule formation, new spectral features appear beyond the linkage zone resonances of the original nuclei. Resonance modes of the molecular bond itself: the new linkage zone formed by the defect-complementarity weaving of surface defects from two or more nuclei possesses its own independent resonant frequencies, typically falling in the infrared region (the scale of the molecular bond linkage zone is larger than that of intra-nuclear linkage zones). Hierarchical coupling modes: the vibration of the molecular bond couples through the shared link network to the resonant frequencies of intra-nuclear scars, producing frequency modulation and leading to the appearance of main peaks and sideband structures. This is the geometric explanation for the emergence of new spectral lines and fine structures in molecular spectra—in the TDMU framework, this is not "electronic energy level transitions" but rather "coupled resonance of linkage zones across different weaving levels."

