8. Conclusion
The atomic nucleus is a continuous topological knot woven layer by layer. During the weaving process, the weaving scars left by the healing of defect-complementarity engagements and the topological defects left unclosed on the surface are the geometric sources of spectral activity.

Spectral line frequency is jointly determined by the linkage zone depth and the global stress state. Deep scars → high local stiffness → high frequency. However, local stiffness is not an isolated property—the stress distribution of the entire weave body can compress or stretch existing scars, altering their resonance frequencies.

Spectral line width is determined by scar smoothness. Low smoothness (rough healed surface) → fast excited-state dissipation → broad line width.

Total number of spectral lines is determined by the total number of scars and defects within the depth accessible to the light's energy. Scars contribute resonant emission, while defects contribute absorption.

Hydrogen: extremely few linkage zones but distributed at all depths → few lines yet covering multiple bands. Iron: extremely numerous linkage zones, uniformly distributed → exceptionally rich lines covering a wide band. Diamond: all surface defects saturation-paired with adjacent nuclei → spectroscopically silent. Superconducting mercury: all linkage zones globally synchronized → low-frequency light is rigidly reflected.

This paper does not use standard-model concepts such as "energy levels," "transitions," "electron orbits," or "photons." All explanations are based on native terminology within the TDMU axiomatic system: weaving scars, topological defects, shared links, orientation transverse wave δ𝒜, link saturation 𝒮, smoothness, and depth.

Appendix: The Status of the Uncertainty Principle in TDMU
The uncertainty relation in standard quantum mechanics is equivalently expressed in TDMU as: the product of the spatial localization precision of a linkage zone (measured in node steps) and the precision of the reconnection-event count history of that zone (measured in reconnection events) has a lower bound determined by the resolution limit of the discrete weave network. This relation is not an a priori postulate of this framework but an emergent statistical property of the discrete combinatorial network in the continuum limit. Its deep reason lies in the fact that each "measurement" (local reconnection probe) of a linkage zone's position inevitably alters the link state of that region, thereby affecting the precise count of its reconnection history.

